==English==

===Etymology===
From Early Modern English {{term||brethren|pos=plural of [[brother]]}}, from {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|brethere|lang=enm}}, {{term|brether|lang=enm}} + {{term|-en|pos=plural ending|lang=en}}, alteration, due to {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|brēþer|pos=dative singular|lang=ang}}, of {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|brōþor}}, {{m|ang|brōþru||brothers, brethren}}. Compare {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Brüder||brothers, brethren|lang=de}}. More at {{l/en|brother}}. The vowel change from ''o'' to ''e'' is called [[w:Germanic umlaut|umlaut]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbrɛðrən/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-brethren.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{head|en|noun plural form}}

# {{context|archaic|lang=en}} {{plural of|brother|lang=en}}
# {{context|figuratively|lang=en}} the body of members, especially of a fraternal, religious or military order

====Usage notes====
The plural {{m|en|brethren}} is generally used for members of an organization, especially a religious body, whereas the plural {{m|en|brothers}} is used in the familial sense as well as for larger groups.

